                <note rend="inline" type="source">124.93/303: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Minister in China</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Johnson</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to
                        the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Nanking</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1935-05-22T16:00:00+08:00">May 22, 1935—4
                        p.m.</date><lb/>[Received May 22—10:07 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>115. Vice Minister for Foreign Affairs today inquired whether I knew the
                    procedure which Department of State intends to follow in bringing about change
                    in status of Legation set forth in Department’s telegram No. 36 of May 15, 7
                    p.m. He asked specifically whether the Department would ask for <hi
                        rendition="#italic">agrément</hi> of new appointee before nomination was
                    made. I stated that I would inquire. Can the Department give me necessary
                    information as to procedure and probable time when plan will be presented? I
                    expect unless otherwise instructed to leave Nanking for Peiping via Shanghai on
                    the night of June 3.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Johnson</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
